ESC: keep minimum throttle signal, add ramping, bench test and BLHeli guide

Two real bugs found while documenting ESC programming:

- esc_init() wrote duty 0, which on OneShot125 means no pulses at all. A
  BLHeli ESC needs to see throttle minimum continuously to arm itself, so
  the ESCs would never arm at power-up. The driver now always emits 125 us
  and "disarmed" only means "not allowed to go above it".
- esc_arm() was only called from the CLI arm command. Arming from the radio
  (the normal path in main.cpp) never enabled the outputs, so the motors
  could not spin. The control loop now syncs esc_arm/esc_disarm with the
  failsafe armed state.

Also:
- esc_protocol: pure module (no hardware) with normalized->pulse mapping,
  slew-rate ramp and range check, so the OneShot125 logic is host-testable
  as the project conventions require. NaN maps to minimum, never to 100%.
- Ramp of 1 us per 1 kHz cycle: 125->250 us in 125 ms, avoiding a step
  that shocks the ESC.
- motortest <1-4> <0-100> [ms] CLI command (max 5 s, refuses while armed)
  to identify motors and check that the four ESCs behave identically.
- Skill esc-setup: new references/blheli-programming.md with the cloning
  procedure (read one, save .ini, write to the other three, verify), the
  recommended BLHeli_S settings for 2205 2300 KV on 4S, and the note that
  OneShot125 needs no throttle calibration.

Tests: new test_esc suite (20 checks). Total 179 checks, all passing.

